<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Porfirio Díaz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a>–<a href="/years/1915">1915</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Porfirio Díaz was a Mexican general, hero of the war against the French intervention, who seized power in 1876 and ruled almost continuously until 1911. The period, called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfiriato">Porfiriato</a>, brought railways, mines and foreign investment but also great concentration of land and repression. His fraudulent re-election in 1910 sparked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a>, and he died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/porfirio-diaz.jpg" alt="Porfirio Díaz" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aurelio Escobar Castellanos, Archive · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Porfirio_diaz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Porfirio Díaz was baptised on 15 September 1830 in the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a>, the sixth of seven children; his exact birth date is unknown. His father, José Faustino Díaz Orozco, was an illiterate workman employed by a firm of merchants, and his mother's mother was Mixtec. In 1845 he entered the seminary of Oaxaca to study for the priesthood, but he later changed to law, with the future president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a> among his mentors. He fought for the Liberals in the Reform War.</p><p>In the second French intervention Díaz fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-puebla">Battle of Puebla</a> in 1862. He was captured after the second siege of the city in 1863 and escaped. As commander in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> he resisted the French until Oaxaca City fell in 1865, and after a second escape he won the Battle of Miahuatlán on 3 October 1866 and retook Oaxaca City. In June 1867 he commanded the forces that took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>When Juárez won the election of 1871 against Díaz, Díaz's supporters alleged fraud and rose in the Plan de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noria">Noria</a>, proclaimed on 8 November 1871. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-tuxtepec">Plan of Tuxtepec</a> of 15 January 1876 opposed the re-election of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebastian-lerdo-de-tejada">Sebastián Lerdo de Tejada</a>, and Díaz took leadership of that rising. He took formal control of the presidency at the start of 1877 and won the election that year. In 1880 he stepped down in favour of his ally Manuel González, but in 1884 he abandoned the principle and held office continuously until 1911.</p><p>Díaz kept power by co-opting and coercing rival interests, and foreign investors from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> were an important support of his regime. Railways financed from abroad opened remote regions to markets. Land surveys gave investors secure title, but they often wiped out the claims of communities that could not prove title, and survey companies kept one-third of the land they measured. Rural communities and small farmers lost their holdings. In 1910 Díaz allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-i-madero">Francisco I. Madero</a> to stand against him, then had him jailed during the election. Madero issued the Plan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a> calling for armed rebellion, and in May 1911, after the Federal Army suffered defeats, Díaz resigned in the Treaty of Ciudad Juárez.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Díaz went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he died of heart failure on 2 July 1915, aged 84. He was buried in the Cimetière du Montparnasse. Before his fall, an adulatory literature called Porfirismo portrayed him internationally as the statesman who had brought stability to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. His legacy has been revised since the 1990s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Porfirio Díaz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Porfirio Díaz</b> foreign capital, much of it American and British, built a railway network of some 19,000 kilometres by 1910, linking mines and plantations to the United States border and the ports, and Mexico became a major producer of silver, copper, henequen and, from the 1900s, oil.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In Latin America the wars of independence were followed by the rule of caudillos, such as José Gaspar Rodríguez de Francia, perpetual dictator of Paraguay from 1816, and <b>Porfirio Díaz</b>, who governed Mexico for most of the years from 1876 to 1911.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Many presidents ruled as caudillos, extended their own terms or were removed by revolts and coups; Mexico’s revolution against the three-decade rule of <b>Porfirio Díaz</b> produced the constitution of 1917, which forbids any president a second term.</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Soldiers of <b>Porfirio Díaz</b>'s army sang of a soldadera who wanted money for the bullfights; for Villa's men, as the historian Elizabeth Salas writes, the cockroach wanted money for alcohol and marijuana.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-cucaracha">La Cucaracha</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Oaxaca de Juárez, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17.06,-96.73&amp;year=1885" aria-label="Oaxaca de Juárez on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1920 1530 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Porfirio Díaz"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2370 1800h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2370 1800h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=17.06,-96.73&amp;year=1885">What was here in 1885? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
